==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|envoke}} {{qual|archaic or nonstandard}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*wekʷ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|*invoken}}, {{m|enm|envoken}}, borrowed from {{der|en|fro|envoquer}}, from {{der|en|la|invocō|invocāre|to call upon}}, itself from {{prefix|la|in-|vocare|t2=to call|nocat=1}}. {{doublet|en|invocate}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|US}} {{enPR|in'vōk}}, {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈvoʊk/}}
* {{a|UK}} {{enPR|in'vōk}}, {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈvəʊk/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-invoke.wav|Audio (UK)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[call]] upon (a [[person]], a [[god]]) for [[help]], [[assistance]] or [[guidance]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[solicit]], petition for, [[appeal]] to a favorable attitude.
#: {{ux|en|The envoy '''invoked''' the King of Kings's magnanimity to reduce his province's tribute after another drought.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1964|month=May|title=News and Comment: Minister hamstrings BR workshops|journal=Modern Railways|page=291|text=Whatever the pressures that have '''invoked''' the Minister's ''diktat'', the outcome is Gilbertian.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2021 August 25|author=David Clough|title=The Sleeper experience|journal=RAIL|issue=938|page=61|text=In May [1995], the court issued an interdict preventing the service withdrawal, pending consultation on the closure to passenger traffic of three short stretches of railway around Glasgow and its hinterland that were only used by the Fort William Sleeper - and for which BR had failed to '''invoke''' standard closure procedures.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To call to mind (something) for some purpose.
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=After marriage, the man had anciently (but this was anterior to Christianity) the power of life and death over his wife. She could '''invoke''' no law against him; he was her sole tribunal and law.|title=[[S:The Subjection of Women|The Subjection of Women]]|author=John Stuart Mill|year=1869}}
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=The acquisition of a useless part can hardly be said to raise an organism in the natural scale; and in the case of the imperfect, closed flowers, above described, if any new principle has to be '''invoked''', it must be one of retrogression rather than of progression; and so it must be with many parasitic and degraded animals.|year=1872|author=Charles Darwin|title=[[S:The Origin of Species (1872)/Chapter VII|The Origin of Species]]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=It is easier to '''invoke''' or to deplore democracy than to say exactly what it is.|year=1912|author=William Sharp McKechnie|title=[[S:The New Democracy and the Constitution|The New Democracy and the Constitution]]}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[appeal]] for validation to a (notably cited) [[authority]].
#: {{ux|en|In certain Christian circles, '''invoking''' the Bible constitutes irrefutable proof.}}
#* '''1969''', Philip Ziegler, ''The Black Death'', Folio Society 2007, p. 21:
#*: He '''invoked''' cadaveric poisoning as the reason for the high death rate among priests and monks {{...}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[conjure]] up with [[incantation]]s.
#: {{ux|en|This satanist ritual '''invokes''' Beelzebub.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[bring about]] as an inevitable consequence.
#: {{ux|en|Blasphemy is taboo as it may '''invoke''' divine wrath.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|computing}} To cause (a [[program]] or [[subroutine]]) to execute.
#: {{ux|en|Interactive programs let the users enter choices and '''invoke''' the corresponding routines.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=August 7, 1974|journal=Computerworld|title=Wholesalers Gain Basic Facts|titleurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=yeSH1uyhLwQC&pg=PA13&lpg=PA12|passage={{...}}the selling price and cost of a particular item are derived by the system through a table lookup and assigned to the item at '''invoking''' time.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Stephen Prata|passage=C++ lets you '''invoke''' an operator function either by calling the function or by using the overloaded operator with its usual syntax.|year=2011|title=C++ Primer Plus}}

